{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgPBL8R7iEL4oLzqyZb5T","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I graded F1 **partly**, under problem **P6** (stock-outs alongside excess stock), and saved the result to `grade.json`.\n\nThe truth schema has no table for P6, so I checked the finding against the key's description of P6 and against the warehouse.\n\n**What the finding gets right:**\n- **Discontinued stock is still bought.** In 2025 the business bought 2,720 units of the 2015-wave equipment and 6,699 units of the 2023-wave equipment for stock, all after their discontinued dates (t366). This is the key's \"ignores discontinued dates… keeps being bought, then marked down.\"\n- **The size checks out.** Marked-down stock lines lost 767,211 (2015 wave) and 2,022,425 (2023 wave) in 2025, which is the finding's 2.79M a year (t367).\n- **The markdown isn't a rep discount.** In the truth table of discounts reps gave beyond the agreed rate, the average is about 0.028 on marked-down and unmarked 2025 equipment lines alike (t368). So the finding is right to rule out H1 and keep this apart from P2.\n\n**Why not full:** P6 has two parts, and the finding covers only the excess-stock part. It doesn't touch the missing seasonal index behind the recurring spring stock-outs, or the short lines left unfilled while other branches had stock.\n\nThe rule's wording fits \"full\" too, since the finding does name P6's discontinued-stock mechanism with evidence. I went with \"partly\" because the rule's \"one part of a two-part problem\" case fits better."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":663,"cache_read_input_tokens":32162,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":663},"output_tokens":512,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics